From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Hughes <ryan@heyoodle.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 21:45:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] iommu: Give external-facing ports the domain of untrusted
 devices

Devices tunneled below an external-facing Root Port are untrusted, and
iommu_get_default_domain_type() gives an untrusted device a DMA domain
even when the default is passthrough. That only works when the device
gets a group of its own. Behind a Root Port without ACS, such as the
USB4 tunnel Root Ports of the NVIDIA N1x, everything plugged in later
joins the Root Port's own group. Its domain was chosen at boot, when
only the trusted port was in it, so with IOMMU_DEFAULT_PASSTHROUGH a
dock's xHCI and Ethernet controller ran with an identity mapping.
Joining an existing group does not go through
iommu_get_default_domain_type(), so nothing noticed.

Choose the domain of an external-facing port as if it were untrusted,
so whatever it carries later is translated, and refuse to probe an
untrusted device that would join a group in an identity domain rather
than give it unrestricted DMA.
---
 drivers/iommu/iommu.c | 30 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/iommu/iommu.c b/drivers/iommu/iommu.c
index 23a5315..4639783 100644
--- a/drivers/iommu/iommu.c
+++ b/drivers/iommu/iommu.c
@@ -667,6 +667,18 @@ static int __iommu_probe_device(struct device *dev, struct list_head *group_list
 	 */
 	list_add_tail(&gdev->list, &group->devices);
 	WARN_ON(group->default_domain && !group->domain);
+	/*
+	 * A device joining a group that already has a domain is not run
+	 * through iommu_get_default_domain_type(). Never let an untrusted one
+	 * share an identity domain.
+	 */
+	if (group->domain && group->domain->type == IOMMU_DOMAIN_IDENTITY &&
+	    dev_is_pci(dev) && to_pci_dev(dev)->untrusted) {
+		dev_err(dev, "Device is not trusted, but IOMMU group %u is in an identity domain, refusing to probe.\n",
+			group->id);
+		ret = -EPERM;
+		goto err_remove_gdev;
+	}
 	if (group->default_domain)
 		iommu_create_device_direct_mappings(group->default_domain, dev);
 	if (group->domain) {
@@ -1872,6 +1884,22 @@ static int iommu_get_def_domain_type(struct iommu_group *group,
 	return cur_type;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Devices plugged in below an external-facing Root Port are untrusted. When
+ * the port does not isolate them with ACS they join the port's own group,
+ * whose domain was chosen before anything was plugged in, so the port needs
+ * the same domain as the untrusted devices it will carry.
+ */
+static bool iommu_dev_needs_untrusted_domain(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct pci_dev *pdev;
+
+	if (!dev_is_pci(dev))
+		return false;
+	pdev = to_pci_dev(dev);
+	return pdev->untrusted || pdev->external_facing;
+}
+
 /*
  * A target_type of 0 will select the best domain type. 0 can be returned in
  * this case meaning the global default should be used.
@@ -1901,7 +1929,7 @@ static int iommu_get_default_domain_type(struct iommu_group *group,
 		driver_type = iommu_get_def_domain_type(group, gdev->dev,
 							driver_type);
 
-		if (dev_is_pci(gdev->dev) && to_pci_dev(gdev->dev)->untrusted) {
+		if (iommu_dev_needs_untrusted_domain(gdev->dev)) {
 			/*
 			 * No ARM32 using systems will set untrusted, it cannot
 			 * work.
